      "text": "To find the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) for which the last two digits of \\( 11 \\cdot t \\) are 36, we need to solve the congruence:\n\\[ 11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}. \\]\n\nFirst, we need to find the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100. This is a number \\( x \\) such that:\n\\[ 11x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}. \\]\n\nWe use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find this inverse. We start by applying the Euclidean Algorithm to 100 and 11:\n\\[ 100 = 9 \\cdot 11 + 1, \\]\n\\[ 11 = 11 \\cdot 1 + 0. \\]\n\nFrom the first equation, we can express 1 as:\n\\[ 1 = 100 - 9 \\cdot 11. \\]\n\nThis tells us that the inverse of 11 modulo 100 is \\(-9\\). Since we want a positive inverse, we convert \\(-9\\) to a positive number by adding 100:\n\\[ -9 + 100 = 91. \\]\n\nThus, the inverse of 11 modulo 100 is 91. Now, we can multiply both sides of the congruence \\( 11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100} \\) by 91:\n\\[ t \\equiv 36 \\cdot 91 \\pmod{100}. \\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\( 36 \\cdot 91 \\):\n\\[ 36 \\cdot 91 = 3276. \\]\n\nNow, we find the last two digits of 3276 by taking \\( 3276 \\mod 100 \\):\n\\[ 3276 \\div 100 = 32 \\text{ remainder } 76. \\]\nSo, \\( 3276 \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100} \\).\n\nTherefore, \\( t \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100} \\), and since \\( t \\) is a two-digit number, the unique solution is:\n\\[ t = 76. \\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{76}. \\]",
