      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find a two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) such that the last two digits of \\( 11 \\cdot t \\) are 36. This can be expressed mathematically as:\n\\[\n11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\nWe need to find \\( t \\) such that \\( t \\) is a two-digit positive integer, i.e., \\( 10 \\leq t \\leq 99 \\).\n\nFirst, we simplify the congruence equation:\n\\[\n11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\nTo solve for \\( t \\), we need the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100. The multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100 is a number \\( x \\) such that:\n\\[\n11x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\nWe can find this inverse using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm. Applying the algorithm to 11 and 100, we get:\n\\[\n100 = 9 \\cdot 11 + 1\n\\]\n\\[\n1 = 100 - 9 \\cdot 11\n\\]\nThis shows that the inverse of 11 modulo 100 is \\(-9\\), which is equivalent to \\(91\\) modulo 100 (since \\(-9 + 100 = 91\\)). Therefore, we have:\n\\[\nt \\equiv 36 \\cdot 91 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\nNow, we compute \\( 36 \\cdot 91 \\):\n\\[\n36 \\cdot 91 = 3276\n\\]\nNext, we find the remainder when 3276 is divided by 100:\n\\[\n3276 \\div 100 = 32 \\text{ remainder } 76\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\n3276 \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\nSo,\n\\[\nt \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\nSince \\( t \\) is a two-digit positive integer, the only possible value for \\( t \\) is 76. We can verify this solution by checking that the last two digits of \\( 11 \\cdot 76 \\) are indeed 36:\n\\[\n11 \\cdot 76 = 836\n\\]\nThe last two digits of 836 are 36, which confirms our solution.\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( t \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{76}\n\\]",
