      "text": "To solve for the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) such that the last two digits of \\( 11 \\cdot t \\) are \\( 36 \\), we need to find \\( t \\) such that:\n\\[ 11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100} \\]\nThis means we need to solve the congruence:\n\\[ 11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100} \\]\nTo do this, we need to find the multiplicative inverse of \\( 11 \\) modulo \\( 100 \\). The multiplicative inverse of \\( 11 \\) modulo \\( 100 \\) is a number \\( x \\) such that:\n\\[ 11x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100} \\]\nWe can find this inverse using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm. First, we apply the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of \\( 11 \\) and \\( 100 \\):\n\\[ 100 = 9 \\cdot 11 + 1 \\]\n\\[ 11 = 11 \\cdot 1 + 0 \\]\nThe gcd is \\( 1 \\), and we can work backwards to express \\( 1 \\) as a combination of \\( 11 \\) and \\( 100 \\):\n\\[ 1 = 100 - 9 \\cdot 11 \\]\nThis tells us that:\n\\[ -9 \\cdot 11 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100} \\]\nThus, the multiplicative inverse of \\( 11 \\) modulo \\( 100 \\) is \\( -9 \\). Since we want a positive inverse, we convert \\( -9 \\) to a positive equivalent by adding \\( 100 \\):\n\\[ -9 + 100 = 91 \\]\nSo, the inverse of \\( 11 \\) modulo \\( 100 \\) is \\( 91 \\). Now we can solve the original congruence by multiplying both sides by \\( 91 \\):\n\\[ t \\equiv 36 \\cdot 91 \\pmod{100} \\]\nWe calculate \\( 36 \\cdot 91 \\):\n\\[ 36 \\cdot 91 = 3276 \\]\nNext, we find the last two digits of \\( 3276 \\) by taking \\( 3276 \\mod 100 \\):\n\\[ 3276 \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100} \\]\nTherefore, \\( t \\) must be \\( 76 \\). We can verify this solution:\n\\[ 11 \\cdot 76 = 836 \\]\nThe last two digits of \\( 836 \\) are \\( 36 \\), which confirms that our solution is correct. Thus, the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{76} \\]",
